WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:01.939
This is lithium.

00:00:01.939 --> 00:00:04.700
Pretty soon, we’re going to need a lot of
it.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.189
Lithium is a useful metal.

00:00:06.189 --> 00:00:11.980
It spends its entire existence trying to get
rid of its one outer electron but, crucially,

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.740
this reaction can be both controlled and reversed.

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:20.210
That means, properly configured, the metal
can discharge energy when needed, take in

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:23.300
more energy, and then discharge that energy.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.300
Essentially, it can act as a battery.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:31.270
It’s only been a few decades since lithium-ion
batteries reached commercial feasibility but,

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:35.160
in that time, they have become the power source
of choice for portable electronics thanks

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.090
to their perfect blend of safety and lightness.

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:43.390
However, the latest major tech boom, the latest
infatuation of Silicon Valley and Wall Street

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:50.040
alike, is centered around the largest consumer
electronics product to date: electric vehicles.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:53.140
And pretty soon, we’re going to need a lot
of them.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:57.930
The UK, for example, has committed to banning
internal combustion car sales by 2030.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:04.720
To replace its 31.5 million vehicles, about
236,000 metric tons of lithium carbonate are

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:05.720
needed.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:10.759
To produce 236,000 metric tons of lithium
carbonate, every lithium mine in the world

00:01:10.759 --> 00:01:15.170
would have to devote its output to this one
use for nine months, and there are a whole

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:19.710
lot more countries, a whole lot more lithium
applications, and a whole lot more growth

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:20.710
in the forecast.

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:25.600
While the industry and its issues may be complex,
the way in which battery-grade lithium is

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.939
produced is not.

00:01:27.939 --> 00:01:32.909
Four countries dominate the industry—Argentina,
Chile, Australia, and China combined account

00:01:32.909 --> 00:01:35.579
for 92% of the globe’s production.

00:01:35.579 --> 00:01:39.350
The metal is extracted from the ground at
massive sites like the Greenbushes mine in

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:43.390
Western Australia, which is the world’s
largest hard-rock lithium mine.

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:47.079
The site was selected due to the abundance
of spodumene in the area, which is a mineral

00:01:47.079 --> 00:01:50.039
that contains large concentrations of lithium.

00:01:50.039 --> 00:01:53.530
Once the raw material is extracted from the
ground, it’s transported two and a half

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:58.040
hours north to the Kwinana Lithium Plant near
Perth—a facility majority owned and operated

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:02.799
by a Chinese company, Tianqi Lithium, which
is responsible for almost half of the world’s

00:02:02.799 --> 00:02:04.090
production of the metal.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:08.630
Once refined, lithium hydroxide and other
compounds are sold to battery manufacturers,

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:13.860
which in about three quarters of cases means
one of three companies—LG Chem, CATL, or

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:15.080
Panasonic.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:18.410
The problem, however, is the world’s solution.

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:23.570
In addition to the UK, Iceland, Belgium, the
Netherlands, Germany, Denmark, Norway, Sweden,

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:27.870
Israel, Singapore, and South Korea have each
committed to banning the sale of internal

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:31.300
combustion passenger vehicles within the next
decade.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:36.160
Adding up their annual passenger vehicle sales
numbers from 2019, that means the absolute

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:41.690
base-case demand for EVs a decade from now
will be 9.5 million per year.

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:45.770
Just to reach that, EV production would have
to quintuple, but even the most conservative

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:51.920
forecasters don’t dare tread anywhere close
to a number as low as 9.5 million in 2032.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:55.730
The market is waking up to what this means
for lithium demand.

00:02:55.730 --> 00:03:01.430
Across 2021, Seaborne lithium prices rose
from around $8,000 per metric ton to over

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:08.510
$30,000—a 400% rise in a mere twelve months—and
lithium is hardly the only crucial metal for

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:12.050
lithium-ion battery production—it’s just
the one in the name.

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:16.150
Cobalt and nickel are also critical to most
commercially-available versions of these batteries,

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:19.530
and the situation is hardly different with
them.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:24.620
Cobalt prices doubled across 2021, while nickel
rose to its highest price in a decade.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:30.510
So, the world needs a lot more metals, but
right now, it’s hard to believe the world’s

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:31.980
going to get them.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:37.710
The biggest hurdle the industry faces is best
exemplified here: Thacker Pass, Nevada.

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:41.860
Thacker Pass is located in one of the most
sparsely populated areas of the country.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:46.620
It’s an half hour’s drive to the nearest
store, an hour to the nearest supermarket,

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:48.910
and three to the nearest Starbucks.

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:53.330
The few roads that exist in the area are lucky
to see a few cars an hour, travelling to and

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:58.720
from the various remote farms, ranches, and
communities dotting northern Nevada.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:03:59.720
That could soon change, though.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:04.210
250 miles or 400 kilometers to the south is
the Silver Peak Lithium Mine.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:08.490
This is the nation’s only currently operating
major lithium mine, despite the fact that

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:13.550
the US is one of the largest EV markets and
home to the world’s largest EV manufacturer.

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:19.090
China, also a major EV market home to major
EV manufacturers, has made significant headway

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:23.020
in building up its domestic lithium production
capacity and the country’s companies also

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:27.289
have significant presences at the world’s
other major lithium production sites.

00:04:27.289 --> 00:04:30.830
This has come to concern those in charge in
the US.

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:36.710
Therefore, sights are set on Thacker Pass—home
to the US’ largest lithium deposit.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:41.500
This site could singlehandedly propel the
US into the ranks of major lithium producers,

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:45.770
but getting a mine up and running there has
proved… difficult.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:51.050
The way in which major lithium deposits are
distributed across the world is rather cruel.

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:55.689
Overwhelmingly, they’re located in arid
regions with little water availability, like

00:04:55.689 --> 00:04:56.689
Nevada.

00:04:56.689 --> 00:05:01.569
Thacker Pass receives less than 10 inches
or 25 centimeters of rain a year.

00:05:01.569 --> 00:05:07.240
However, the extraction and processing of
lithium requires enormous quantities of water.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:13.289
It’s expected that operations at the proposed
Thacker Pass lithium mine would require 3,224

00:05:13.289 --> 00:05:20.150
gallons or 12,204 liters of water per minute—roughly
equivalent to the contents of a backyard,

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:21.150
above-ground pool.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:24.849
That water would be used to pump into the
ground as part of the extraction process,

00:05:24.849 --> 00:05:29.270
during refinement, and to conduct necessary
dust control at the site.

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:33.479
To get this water the mine would have to pump
it out of the ground using wells, but every

00:05:33.479 --> 00:05:38.910
acre-foot of water in the area is strictly
allocated, given the degree of scarcity.

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:43.319
So the mine has to buy up water rights from
others in order to gain the legal right to

00:05:43.319 --> 00:05:44.440
use it.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:49.080
What that means, however, is that there’s
a direct trade off between one use and another,

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:54.219
and in this case, the other use is predominantly
ranching and farming—two key tenants to

00:05:54.219 --> 00:05:55.849
the local economy.

00:05:55.849 --> 00:06:00.069
In addition, there’s a chance the project
could do far more to further the inaccessibility

00:06:00.069 --> 00:06:01.160
of water in northern Nevada.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:05.909
The US Bureau of Land Management’s Environmental
Impact Study for the project found that it

00:06:05.909 --> 00:06:10.740
presented the distinct possibility of leaking
unacceptable levels of arsenic into the area’s

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:15.180
groundwater table which could take the entire
region’s water supply offline for hundreds

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:16.210
of years.

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:20.939
In an area where the availability of water
undergirds almost all economic activity, that

00:06:20.939 --> 00:06:23.840
has people seriously concerned.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:26.800
The issues only compound on top of that.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:31.610
As Thacker Pass is, of course, a mountain
pass, it acts as a wildlife corridor between

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:35.780
the Double-H and Montana mountains—two biodiversity
hotspots.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:40.499
Therefore, the environmental impact study
found the project likely to destroy or deteriorate

00:06:40.499 --> 00:06:45.419
thousands of acres of habitat used by the
pronghorn antelope, sage grouse, golden eagle,

00:06:45.419 --> 00:06:47.120
and other unique species.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:52.059
For interrelated reasons, the project also
has a number of local indigenous tribes concerned—the

00:06:52.059 --> 00:06:55.750
most vocal of which is the Fort McDermitt
Paiute and Shoshone Tribe.

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:59.599
They say that during the era of American soldiers
rounding up and shipping indigenous people

00:06:59.599 --> 00:07:03.909
off to reservations, two of the tribe’s
families hid out in the shelter Thacker Pass

00:07:03.909 --> 00:07:07.930
provided—so they directly attribute the
continued existence of their tribe to the

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:08.930
area.

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:13.430
In addition, they consider the pass a sacred
site, in part because of a historic massacre

00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:15.069
they say occurred there.

00:07:15.069 --> 00:07:19.060
This assertion, however, was directly challenged
in a court case related to the mine project,

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:22.779
and the judge rejected the claim citing a
lack of evidence.

00:07:22.779 --> 00:07:26.439
To add to their opposition, the tribe put
forward evidence linking the development of

00:07:26.439 --> 00:07:31.020
similar resource-extraction projects, which
are predominately staffed by men, to increases

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:34.849
in the rape and murder of indigenous women
in nearby areas.

00:07:34.849 --> 00:07:39.569
Even just looking at these few headline issues,
it becomes clear that the Thacker Pass lithium

00:07:39.569 --> 00:07:45.229
mine project is mired in a nearly insurmountable
web of controversy and conflict, and it’s

00:07:45.229 --> 00:07:47.410
hardly alone in that status.

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:51.499
Much of the evidence opponents to the Thacker
Pass mine have put forward is based on real-world

00:07:51.499 --> 00:07:56.349
experiences in the lithium triangle—the
nexus between Chile, Argentina, and Bolivia

00:07:56.349 --> 00:07:59.529
that hosts some of the world’s most productive
lithium production facilities.

00:07:59.529 --> 00:08:04.509
An area in a similar situation—a remote,
arid landscape punctuated by small communities

00:08:04.509 --> 00:08:08.479
home to a historically oppressed indigenous
population—the lithium triangle has seen

00:08:08.479 --> 00:08:13.319
an economic boon, but it’s come at the cost
of environmental and cultural devastation.

00:08:13.319 --> 00:08:17.729
Just as the issues are not confined to one
geography, they’re not even confined to

00:08:17.729 --> 00:08:19.309
lithium alone.

00:08:19.309 --> 00:08:24.259
Some 70% of the world’s cobalt, a crucial
component to current battery tech, comes from

00:08:24.259 --> 00:08:28.339
the Democratic Republic of the Congo—the
8th poorest country in the world, according

00:08:28.339 --> 00:08:29.590
to World Bank figures.

00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:34.130
While a majority of the cobalt mining is conducted
by large mining companies with often shaky

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:38.930
safety and human rights records, a concerningly
large minority is accomplished through what’s

00:08:38.930 --> 00:08:43.650
referred to as “artisanal” mining—a
term defining the illegal, informal practice

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:48.000
of individuals mining cobalt by themselves
and selling it on to shady middlemen.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:52.709
The complete lack of safety standards or regulations
in the sector means child labor and deadly

00:08:52.709 --> 00:08:54.520
mine collapses are rampant.

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:59.370
For those that aren’t directly injured or
killed on the job, long-term exposure to cobalt

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:03.570
mines has been linked to significant health
effects later in life, and fatal birth defects

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:05.020
for children in the region.

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:09.040
Altogether, there’s almost no such thing
as ethical cobalt.

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:12.320
There’s also almost no such thing as green
lithium.

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:16.640
There’s little appetite anywhere to increasing
lithium mining in the places where it’s

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:21.270
accessible, and little progress has been made
in the DRC in making cobalt mining less socially

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:22.270
disastrous.

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:28.750
As demand for EVs and their batteries increases,
getting more cobalt and lithium will be incredibly

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:29.750
difficult.

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:34.550
However, on top of that, getting more cobalt
and lithium that’s more ethical and green,

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:38.760
or even as ethical and green, will be next
to impossible.

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:43.660
But to decarbonize driving, solutions must
be found.

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:47.820
One option, rather than finding more raw materials,
is to need less of them.

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:51.870
Of course, the way to do that is by making
batteries better.

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:56.600
The most promising short-term innovation that
could fulfill that mission is solid state

00:09:56.600 --> 00:09:58.019
batteries.

00:09:58.019 --> 00:10:03.220
Whereas traditional EV batteries have a liquidy,
viscous lithium-based electrolyte, solid state

00:10:03.220 --> 00:10:08.339
batteries rather use a solid, metal composition
as their ion transport mechanism.

00:10:08.339 --> 00:10:12.310
This switch has a number of benefits including
a higher safety profile that reduces the risk

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:16.769
of fire, and therefore reduces the need for
expensive safety features.

00:10:16.769 --> 00:10:20.920
Solid state batteries can also be made without
cobalt or nickel, which eliminates two problematic

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:24.220
and costly necessities in current battery
tech.

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:29.089
Most significant, however, is solid state
batteries’ higher energy density.

00:10:29.089 --> 00:10:33.920
Traditional lithium ion compositions used
in EV battery packs store about 114 watt-hours

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:37.740
of energy per pound, or 250 per kilogram.

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:42.780
That means one pound of battery could move
a Tesla Model 3, for example, 0.4 miles, or

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:44.860
1 kilogram 1.3 kilometers.

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:49.370
Meanwhile, it’s expected that solid state
batteries will be able to store between 175

00:10:49.370 --> 00:10:55.860
and 225 watt-hours per pound or 400 to 500
per kilogram—essentially doubling battery

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:56.860
density.

00:10:56.860 --> 00:11:00.819
That means Tesla could halve the weight of
their half-ton battery pack and not only keep

00:11:00.819 --> 00:11:05.110
range the same, but increase it as the car
would no longer need to carry the rest of

00:11:05.110 --> 00:11:06.880
the weight of the battery pack.

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:11.649
On top of all those benefits, expert believe
that, at scale, production costs of solid

00:11:11.649 --> 00:11:16.069
state batteries could be even less than the
cheapest current lithium-ion batteries.

00:11:16.069 --> 00:11:18.920
However, the issue is getting to that scale.

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:23.829
Battery production needs to occur at absolutely
massive quantities to reach cost competitiveness—an

00:11:23.829 --> 00:11:27.790
assertion backed up by the industry’s current
effective triopoly.

00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:31.990
The process of working down this cost curve
is long as there are few applications where

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:36.720
battery weight matters as much as with EVs,
and EVs won’t switch to solid state batteries

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:40.410
until their cost is competitive, but their
cost will only become competitive when the

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:44.480
industry reaches a production capacity that
only EVs can provide.

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:48.889
So, the industry has to wait for some level
of scale to occur through niche solid state

00:11:48.889 --> 00:11:53.450
battery applications in medical devices, race
cars, and fighter jets; then wait for consumer

00:11:53.450 --> 00:11:57.180
electronics to realize the weight savings
or battery life benefits the innovation could

00:11:57.180 --> 00:12:01.410
provide; then wait for the highest-end EVs
to incorporate the technology in order to

00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:05.959
offer super-long ranges as a luxury; before
solid-state batteries can finally reach a

00:12:05.959 --> 00:12:09.860
cost that would allow them to permeate into
what will by then be the large segment of

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:11.850
everyday EVs.

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:16.389
Most estimates place that enticing end-goal
more than a decade away.

00:12:16.389 --> 00:12:20.639
Even if the solid-state battery transition
reaches fruition earlier, the world will still

00:12:20.639 --> 00:12:24.389
need a whole lot more lithium.

00:12:24.389 --> 00:12:29.459
Far from the potential environmental disaster
at Thacker Pass is an existing environmental

00:12:29.459 --> 00:12:31.759
disaster—the Salton Sea.

00:12:31.759 --> 00:12:37.149
A century ago, Colorado River floodwaters
breached through an irrigation canal and accumulated,

00:12:37.149 --> 00:12:43.170
over years, in the Imperial Valley’s geographic
low-point 236 feet or 72 meters below sea

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:44.170
level.

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:48.220
That massive puddle still exists today, but
some of the water has slowly evaporated through

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:53.339
time, leaving an ever saltier, dirtier accumulation
of water.

00:12:53.339 --> 00:12:57.639
Thousands more feet below, however, are a
number of underground volcanoes that superheat

00:12:57.639 --> 00:12:59.480
water to hundreds of degrees.

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:03.920
If one brings that water to the surface, the
pressure change leads to it transforming into

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:09.839
steam and steam, of course, is what most power
plants use to drive turbines.

00:13:09.839 --> 00:13:14.040
Traditional power plants use coal or natural
gas to heat water up into steam, but this

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:16.870
steam is created by the earth—meaning its
carbon-free.

00:13:16.870 --> 00:13:22.269
That’s why Berkshire Hathaway Energy has
built 10 geothermal energy plants in the area,

00:13:22.269 --> 00:13:27.790
but, crucially, this superheated water is
filled with something else: lithium.

00:13:27.790 --> 00:13:31.910
Therefore, these geothermal plants are planning
on adding an extra step in their process to

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:34.839
extract lithium from the briny steam they
use.

00:13:34.839 --> 00:13:39.410
Now, there are certainly significant technological
hurdles that stand between now and a future

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:43.360
of commercially-competitive lithium production
at the Salton Sea, especially as the metal

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:48.529
only represents a tiny portion of the slurry
of materials found in the water, but the lithium

00:13:48.529 --> 00:13:50.240
is there.

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:53.939
Berkshire Hathaway Energy, as the largest
existing energy company working around the

00:13:53.939 --> 00:13:58.420
Salton Sea, is leading the charge thanks in
part to a sizable federal grant, and expects

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:02.439
to have its demonstration facility up and
running later in 2022.

00:14:02.439 --> 00:14:05.899
A number of other competitors have already
started developing their lithium-extraction

00:14:05.899 --> 00:14:11.040
plays around the Salton Sea, meaning America’s
first lithium boom-town might already be a

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:13.370
foregone conclusion.

00:14:13.370 --> 00:14:18.199
These are the kind of solutions needed as
the world transitions to electric mobility.

00:14:18.199 --> 00:14:22.380
Electric vehicles, due to their reliance on
batteries, are just dirtier than internal

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:24.950
combustion vehicles to produce.

00:14:24.950 --> 00:14:30.570
That being said, the vast majority of emissions
from cars, including from EVs themselves,

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:33.860
come not from the production of vehicles but
from driving them.

00:14:33.860 --> 00:14:39.930
The science on the issue is sound—electric
vehicles, from production to use to scrapping,

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:45.569
are responsible for about 75% less emissions
than their internal combustion counterparts,

00:14:45.569 --> 00:14:48.970
even on current, fossil-fuel based electric
grids.

00:14:48.970 --> 00:14:53.879
Anyone who argues the opposite is either misinformed
or attempting to disinform, and that gap will

00:14:53.879 --> 00:14:56.930
only widen as grids continue to decarbonize.

00:14:56.930 --> 00:15:01.569
However, there can be better alternatives
to better alternatives.

00:15:01.569 --> 00:15:06.660
In the coming lithium gold-rush, corners can
and likely will be cut.

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:10.390
The question the world will have to grapple
with is whether it’s worth destroying pristine

00:15:10.390 --> 00:15:14.970
environments like Thacker Pass in the name
of environmentalism—whether slowing the

00:15:14.970 --> 00:15:19.459
issue on a global level is worth accelerating
it on a local level.

00:15:19.459 --> 00:15:23.810
Then, when the answer inevitably gravitates
towards yes, the world will have to grapple

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:26.350
with who must confront that local devastation.

00:15:26.350 --> 00:15:30.740
If the answer continues to be to place the
burden on the world’s most vulnerable, then

00:15:30.740 --> 00:15:35.430
even if the steady march of climate change
is curbed, will the world have truly succeeded

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:37.089
in stopping its effects?

00:15:37.089 --> 00:15:41.029
Is it worth sending teenagers to die in a
war to prevent the propagation of terrorist

00:15:41.029 --> 00:15:42.269
organizations?

00:15:42.269 --> 00:15:45.610
Is it worth depressing an economy to slow
the spread of a pandemic?

00:15:45.610 --> 00:15:49.790
It it worth allowing individuals to own guns
for self-protection even if it allows easier

00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:51.650
access for bad actors?

00:15:51.650 --> 00:15:57.029
Determining what is an acceptable sacrifice
has historically led to some of the most contentious,

00:15:57.029 --> 00:15:59.570
perennial political issues in history.

00:15:59.570 --> 00:16:05.649
Here, it’s happening again: the public needs
to decide, with the fate of the world on top

00:16:05.649 --> 00:16:11.060
of them, how much bad should be allowed for
the greater good.

